<article class="env-definition" id="004P">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/004P">Definition <span data-tag="004P">5.5.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a topological space. A collection of subsets $\mathcal{B}$ of $X$ is called a <em>base for the topology on $X$</em> or a <em>basis for the topology on $X$</em> if the following conditions hold: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Every element $B \in \mathcal{B}$ is open in $X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every open $U \subset X$ and every $x \in U$, there exists an element $B \in \mathcal{B}$ such that $x \in B \subset U$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>